  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/e79856c97ab7e4dd27bb1f476fd911c2e7d77aac6ab54fe50597b3aafbca720c.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,\u2216D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,\u2216\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III are equals $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
